.TH fabrics.conf "5" "@MONTH@ @YEAR@" "mhvtl @VERSION@" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
fabrics.conf \- which initiators may reach which mhvtl library
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fabrics.conf
is read by
.BR mhvtl-fabrics (1).
It uses the format of
.BR device.conf (5):
a record starts in column 1, its fields are indented \fBName: value\fR lines,
and a \fB#\fR after a blank starts a comment. Field names ignore case.
Unknown records and fields are errors.
.PP
Field names follow the iSCSI terms of RFC 7143.
.SH RECORDS
.TP
.B VERSION: 1
Optional; the format version.
.TP
.B iSCSI:
Settings for every iSCSI target.
.RS
.TP
.BI "Portal: " address [: port ]
Address to listen on, repeatable. An IPv6 address goes in brackets, e.g.
\fB[2001:db8::10]:3260\fR. The port defaults to 3260. Without any Portal the
targets listen on \fB0.0.0.0:3260\fR. A wildcard address and a specific
address of the same family cannot share a port.
.TP
.BI "Base name: " iqn
Prefix of the target names. Required unless every \fBiSCSI target\fR record
has a \fBTarget name\fR. There is no default: an iSCSI name's naming
authority must be a domain its owner controls (RFC 7143 section 4.2.7.4).
The sample configuration uses \fBiqn.2003-01.org.linux-iscsi.\fIhost\fB:mhvtl\fR,
the prefix LIO's targetcli generates; put this host's name in place of
\fIhost\fR so names stay unique, or use a domain you own.
.RE
.TP
.BI "iSCSI target: " ID
Exports the library (or standalone drive) with this ID in device.conf.
A drive that belongs to a library is exported with its library. The target
is named \fIBase name\fB.lib\fIID\fR (\fB.tape\fIID\fR for a standalone
drive).
.RS
.TP
.BI "Initiator name: " name
iSCSI name allowed to log in, repeatable. A record without one is not
exported.
.TP
.BI "Target name: " name
Overrides the default target name.
.TP
.BI "CHAP name: " name ", CHAP secret: " secret
Every initiator of this record must authenticate with CHAP.
.TP
.BI "Mutual CHAP name: " name ", Mutual CHAP secret: " secret
The target authenticates to the initiator too. RFC 7143 section 9.2.1
requires a secret different from the CHAP secret.
.RE
.PP
Names must be lower case \fBiqn.\fR, \fBeui.\fR or \fBnaa.\fR names. CHAP
names and secrets are printable characters without blanks. RFC 7143 section
9.2.1 asks for secrets of at least 96 bits, 12 characters; some initiators
require 12 to 16.
.SH SECURITY
An initiator name is an identity, not a credential: any host can claim it.
Without CHAP, a listed name gets full read and write access to the tapes,
so use CHAP unless the portals are on a trusted storage network.
.PP
The file must be owned by root (or the user running mhvtl-fabrics) and not
writable by group or others. When it holds a secret it must be mode 0600.
Otherwise it is refused.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
VERSION: 1

iSCSI:
 Portal: 192.0.2.10:3260
 Base name: iqn.2003-01.org.linux-iscsi.vtl1:mhvtl

iSCSI target: 10
 Initiator name: iqn.2024-01.com.example:pbs1
 CHAP name: pbs1
 CHAP secret: change-me-0123
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mhvtl-fabrics (1),
.BR device.conf (5)
